{"id": "ev-c7c0330b7d9d", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/cnsm.2013.6727803", "date": "2013-10-01", "coverage": "A scalable approach for structuring large-scale hierarchical cloud management systems", "excerpt": "In recent years, the scale of clouds and networks has increased greatly. It is important to ensure that the management systems used in these environments can scale as well. A centralized system does not scale well, while for distributed approaches, it is difficult to maintain an overview of the global system state. In hierarchical management systems, nodes at a low level in the hierarchy have a de", "limitation": "网页存档（exa-contents），只用于所引数字/事实；日期为页面发布或检索所示日期，未注明者为 null", "local_archive": "data/sources/c7c0330b7d9d.json", "sha256": "ef09722f34300e5216b4e81d032639b5952d15f6fe71ecfac2751445f094bbad", "verified": true}
{"id": "ev-9804ab35d8f5", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1504/ijcnds.2024.135092", "date": "2023-12-01", "coverage": "Developing policy hierarchies for an effective distributed systems and network management: a case study in a videoconference service", "excerpt": "Large-scale distribution systems and network management depend on complex relationships that are strongly related to the managerial roles, which depend on the management policies set. Management policies express peer-to-peer or hierarchical management relationships which constitute the policy hierarchies. Policy hierarchies must satisfy at the highest management level, the business goals, while th", "limitation": "网页存档（exa-contents），只用于所引数字/事实；日期为页面发布或检索所示日期，未注明者为 null", "local_archive": "data/sources/9804ab35d8f5.json", "sha256": "623ebc5f896fc259f5acf7b07278eb163cc8aa3fa37d3d43073b68a36f484e22", "verified": true}
{"id": "ev-2296ffdbfd92", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1109/acsos-c58168.2023.00042", "date": "2023-09-25", "coverage": "Hierarchy Beyond Top-Down Control: The Architecture of Self-Organised Social Systems", "excerpt": "Diverse social system architectures are needed to address the inter-related social and environmental challenges that societies face across scales. The iron law of oligarchy states that all societies eventually become hierarchical, with top-down control, as they grow in size and complexity. We contribute to existing research arguing that this is not an inevitability. To do this, we analyse social s", "limitation": "网页存档（exa-contents），只用于所引数字/事实；日期为页面发布或检索所示日期，未注明者为 null", "local_archive": "data/sources/2296ffdbfd92.json", "sha256": "4c3e66a2d5069001126f5bb14b4fb64b74827b22ac185eb743257ece2b23ed55", "verified": true}
